---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Capabilities" description="SIGRUN's object-capability system: per-process tables, rights bitmasks, derivation that can only narrow, move/copy/loan transfer, and revocation.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Subsystem 8 · capabilities</p>
  <h1>Capabilities: unforgeable references with rights</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This is the security model that gives the project its name. Instead of asking "is this process allowed to
    do X?", SIGRUN asks "does this process hold a capability for X?" — a kernel-minted handle that carries its
    own rights.
  </p>

  <h2>The model in three properties</h2>
  <div class="pitch">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Unforgeable</h3>
      <p>
        A capability is created only by the kernel (a fresh <code class="mono">CapabilityId</code> from an
        atomic counter) and stored in a per-process table. User code can hold it and pass it — it cannot
        manufacture one, because it has no way to allocate a slot the kernel didn't fill.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Rights-carrying</h3>
      <p>
        The rights live <em>in</em> the capability, not in a table somewhere else. A read-only capability for a
        frame and a read-write one for the same frame are different objects with different authority.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Monotonically narrowing</h3>
      <p>
        Deriving a new capability can only <em>intersect</em> rights with the parent's. You can hand out less
        authority than you have, never more — which is what makes confinement provable rather than policed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

