  <h2>The table</h2>
  <p>
    Each process owns a <code class="mono">CapabilityTable</code> mapping <code class="mono">SlotId</code> to
    capability, with a free-list so removed slots are reused. A slot is the userspace-visible handle —
    like a file descriptor, except that the object behind it is typed and rights-bearing.
  </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// capability/table.rs</span>
pub struct CapabilityTable &#123;
    slots: BTreeMap&lt;SlotId, Capability&gt;,
    free_slots: Vec&lt;SlotId&gt;,        <span class="c">// reused on the next insert</span>
    next_slot: u32,
    owner: u64,
&#125;
pub fn derive(&amp;mut self, parent_slot: SlotId, new_rights: CapRights)
    -&gt; Result&lt;SlotId, CapError&gt; &#123;
    let parent = self.lookup(parent_slot)?;
    if !parent.rights.contains(CapRights::GRANT) &#123;
        return Err(CapError::RightsViolation);          <span class="c">// no delegation right</span>
    &#125;
    if !parent.rights.contains(new_rights) &#123;
        return Err(CapError::CannotDeriveMoreRights);   <span class="c">// cannot amplify</span>
    &#125;
    self.insert(Capability &#123; id: CapabilityId::new(),
        object_type: parent.object_type, object_id: parent.object_id,
        rights: new_rights &#125;)                            <span class="c">// same object, fewer rights</span>
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code class="mono">check_rights(slot, required)</code> is the enforcement primitive; the
    <code class="mono">AuthorizedOperation</code> trait turns it into typed checks —
    <code class="mono">ReadOp::is_authorized(&amp;cap)</code>, <code class="mono">GrantOp</code>, and so on — so
    call sites name the operation instead of assembling a bitmask.
  </p>

  <h2>Three ways to hand over authority</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Source keeps it?</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="mono">Move</td><td><span class="badge dead">NO</span></td><td>Removed from the source table, inserted into the target — authority <em>transfers</em>. The safe default for passing a handle you're done with.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="mono">Copy</td><td><span class="badge work">YES</span></td><td>The capability is cloned; both tables now hold equal authority. Note both derive from the same object, so each can be narrowed independently.</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="mono">Loan</td><td><span class="badge work">YES</span></td><td>A revocable grant: the borrower gets a capability, and the lender can <code class="mono">revoke_capability</code> later — the borrower's copy disappears.</td></tr>
  </table>
<pre><code><span class="c">// capability/transfer.rs — the three modes, abridged</span>
TransferMode::Move =&gt; &#123;
    let cap = source.remove(source_slot)?;
    let target_slot = target.insert(cap)?;                 <span class="c">// authority leaves the source</span>
&#125;
TransferMode::Copy =&gt; &#123;
    let cap = source.lookup(source_slot)?.clone();
    let target_slot = target.insert(cap)?;                 <span class="c">// both retain it</span>
&#125;
TransferMode::Loan =&gt; &#123;
    let loaned = Capability &#123; id: CapabilityId::new(), ..*original &#125;;  <span class="c">// revocable</span>
    let target_slot = target.insert(loaned)?;
&#125;</code></pre>
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="label">Capabilities ride inside IPC messages</span>
    <code class="mono">Message::with_cap(id)</code> puts up to four capability ids inline, marks the message
    <code class="mono">CAP_TRANSFER</code>, and <code class="mono">CapabilityMessage</code> pairs a 64-byte body
    with a list of <code class="mono">(SlotId, TransferMode)</code>. That's the seL4 "grant" operation: you
    don't send a pointer, you send the authority itself.
  </div>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <span class="label">Honest gaps in the capability system</span>
    Rights are checked at the API boundary, but the objects behind capabilities (frames, endpoints) don't yet
    consult the table before being used — so this is a working data model, not yet an enforcement boundary.
    <code class="mono">derive_capability()</code> at the top of <code class="mono">capability/mod.rs</code> is a
    stub returning a hardcoded object. Revocation is shallow: it finds a capability for the same object in the
    borrower's table, but there's no stored derivation tree, so it cannot walk a chain
    (derive → derive → derive) — the classic hard part of capability systems.
  </div>

  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Interview Q&amp;A: capabilities</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Capabilities vs ACLs — what's the difference and what does each cost you?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          An ACL attaches a list of subjects to the object and checks it at access time; a capability attaches
          the authority to the subject and needs no lookup. Capabilities make delegation natural (hand over the
          token), make confinement provable (you can't use authority you weren't given), and make the fast path
          cheap (one indexing operation). The cost is revocation — with no central list, "take away X's access"
          means walking every holder, or using indirection. ACLs revoke trivially and delegate awkwardly. SIGRUN
          chose capabilities, which is why revoke is the weakest part of the story.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "How do real capability systems revoke?" — Three broad options: track a derivation tree so revocation walks descendants (expensive but precise); use indirection objects (revoke by swapping the object behind a handle); or use epochs/generations so stale capabilities fail a check. seL4 tracks a CDT (capability derivation tree).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Why does <code class="mono">derive</code> require the GRANT right?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          To make delegation explicitly grantable. If any holder could derive, then a process that received a
          read-only capability could produce more read-only capabilities and hand them out — authority would leak
          by default. Requiring <code class="mono">GRANT</code> means the original owner decides whether this
          holder may delegate, which is the difference between "you can read this" and "you can decide who else
          reads this". It's a two-bit distinction that turns out to matter a lot.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>Why a <code class="mono">BTreeMap</code> of slots rather than a fixed array of 1024?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Flexibility at low cost: most processes hold a handful of capabilities, so a sparse map avoids
          reserving (and zeroing) 1024 entries per process, while still giving O(log n) lookup and — importantly —
          a natural free-list so slot indices are recycled. The trade is that the index is not a dense
          file-descriptor-like table, and iterating all capabilities is a tree walk. A production kernel would
          likely split the difference: a small inline array for the first N slots, spilling to a map.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">DESIGN</span>What would break first if this became a real security boundary?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Enforcement at the object, not the table. Today a capability is a record in a table, and the code that
          maps a frame or writes to an endpoint doesn't consult that record — so the permission is advisory.
          The fix is that every object access goes through the capability (the endpoint *is* the capability, as
          in seL4, rather than an id that points to one), plus a derivation tree for precise revocation and
          reference counting so an object dies when its last capability does. Those are the three things I'd
          build next.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "Is there a reference-counting bug here?" — Potentially: <code class="mono">derive</code> mints a new id for the same object without recording the parent link, and there is no per-object capability refcount, so "last capability dropped ⇒ free the object" can't be derived from the current structures.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

